{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEMeUPn2Sq2Trbszk2JX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've fixed the integration. It works end to end against a local mock of Intervals.icu, but I haven't tried it against the real service: that needs a real account and the real client credentials, and I made no external calls. The causes and fixes are written up in `ANSWER.md`.\n\n**Causes:**\n1. **The Connect button got a 401 for everyone.** It was a plain link to `GET /api/intervals/auth/init`. Opening a link doesn't send the app's sign-in token, which only goes out with the app's own API calls, so the server always replied \"unauthorized\". Init is now an authenticated `POST` that returns `{authorize_url}`, and the button sends the browser there (`static/app.js`, `server.py`).\n2. **Pasting the authorize link stopped working after calendar sync shipped.** The requested scopes included `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE` and `CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`. Intervals allows each area only once, because WRITE already includes READ. The scope is now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE` (`config.py`). This one is inferred: the captured 400 only says `invalid_request` without naming the bad parameter. But the scope is what changed, and it breaks the one-per-area rule.\n3. **Rivera was told to reconnect after an hour.** Intervals tokens never expire and come with no refresh token. Our code assumed a 3600 s lifetime when none was given, and a background \"refresher\" then threw away a token that was still valid. I removed all the expiry and refresh code. Now a 401 from Intervals clears the token and shows \"revoked, reconnect\".\n\n**Also changed, following your guide:**\n- The token exchange sends only `client_id`, `client_secret` and `code`.\n- The `state` parameter is now a signed token naming the user and its purpose, valid for 15 minutes. The callback checks it before exchanging the code.\n- If a reconnect comes back with a different Intervals athlete than the one already linked, it's rejected.\n\n**Mock test results:** init by POST returns the corrected scope, and the old GET route now returns 404. The callback stores the connection, and a tampered state is rejected. Wellness data loads, a different athlete on reconnect gets a 409, and a 401 from Intervals moves the athlete to \"reconnect\".\n\n**Still to do:**\n- Run the guide's live checks with a real account: connect and load wellness, revoke the app in Intervals and confirm the app asks to reconnect, and confirm a duplicated scope is rejected.\n- Encrypt the access token in the production database. It's a credential that never expires; the dev server only keeps it in memory.\n- Rivera will need to reconnect once. Since the new scope adds calendar write access, Intervals will probably show the consent screen again."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":115,"cache_read_input_tokens":40012,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":115},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14dffb46-c88e-4d30-8c8b-5da3b3530eac","uuid":"273740fb-21f4-4329-b1ed-2c6c560b84ed","timestamp":"2026-10-20T23:28:32.019Z","request_id":"req_011CgEMeU8Q6RsGogv8CmHDp"}
